# Template for .private-data-local — copy it, then fill in your own values:
#
#   cp .private-data-local.example .private-data-local
#
# .private-data-local is gitignored, because on a public repo the denylist would
# itself be the leak. Everything here is a POSIX extended regex, one per line
# (a plain literal is a valid regex); matching is case-insensitive. `#` comments
# and blank lines are ignored.
#
# The scanner already derives two things at runtime with no configuration —
# your home directory path and your git `user.email` — so those need no entry.
#
# Add the terms that a scrub would have to remove from this tree. Past leaks in
# this repo were: the operator's real name and family names, the production
# hostname, the live bot's mail domain, brokerage account numbers, and the real
# user id used as a test identity.

# --- People ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Real first/last names used as placeholders in examples or fixtures.
# \bJane Roe\b
# \bRoe\b

# --- Hosts and domains ----------------------------------------------------
# Production hostnames and any internal domain. Include the bare label so a
# hostname like "prod-01.internal" is caught by "internal" alone.
# \bprod-01\.example-internal\.net\b
# \bexample-internal\b

# --- Accounts and identifiers --------------------------------------------
# Brokerage/bank account numbers, real user ids, device ids.
# \bZ[0-9]{8}\b
# \bmy-real-user-id\b

# --- Places ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Home address fragments, hometown, employer.
# \b123 Real Street\b
